From 9544ad95c2a0f03b00310bbd3b078d275a7f7e35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: moritztng <moritz.thuening@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 15:10:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Find and pin design workers without nvidia-smi

A campaign enumerated its accelerators with nvidia-smi and pinned each worker
with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES, so on a machine installed with the rocm or oneapi
extra visible_design_gpus() returned nothing, plan_design_workers() built an
empty plan, and the campaign ran one process on one device however many the
node held. Device memory was unreadable there too, which held even a single
card to one worker.

Enumeration now reads CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES or
ZE_AFFINITY_MASK, then nvidia-smi, and falls back to jax.devices() with the
host CPU left out. Memory falls back to a device's PJRT memory_stats(). Each
worker is pinned with the variable its own runtime reads, chosen from the
device platform, resolving JAX's shared "gpu" name by which plugin is
installed. JAX's own jax_cuda_visible_devices and jax_rocm_visible_devices are
absl flags rather than environment states, so they do not reach a worker
process and are not used.

Both nvidia-smi calls are tried first and unchanged, so on an NVIDIA machine
the devices found, their order, their memory and the variable each worker is
pinned with are what they were, and no JAX backend is initialised any earlier
than before. Where a platform offers no way to pin a worker the campaign says
so and designs on one device, rather than fanning out onto a device every
worker would share.

tests/test_design_workers.py covers both paths with stub devices. Nine of its
tests describe behaviour this commit preserves and pass at either revision.
